WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.820
ARMAN HEZARKHANI: Hi, my
name is Arman Hezarkhani.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.370
And this is an introduction
to Google Cloud.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.620
This video is intended
for use by anyone

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.410
interested in preparing
for their future careers.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.970
In the next few
minutes, we're going

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.250
to discuss what Google
Cloud is and what

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.950
some student developers have
been able to create with it.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:17.700
Ready?

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:19.668
We're going to start
by going back in time.

00:00:19.668 --> 00:00:21.210
Let's pretend for
a moment that we're

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.090
in the time before
cloud computing,

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.590
and we want to start a company
to create a social networking

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:26.580
site.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.490
You'd start by buying a
server, lugging it all the way

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.560
home from the store,
and spending hours

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.960
preparing it with all the
correct hardware and software

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.073
so it can run your
company's web app.

00:00:37.073 --> 00:00:39.240
Fast forward a few months,
and things are looking up

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.440
for your company's site.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.480
The users are flooding in.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.400
As your organization's
user base grows,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.220
you have to buy and install
more and more servers.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.440
This is called scaling.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.020
Eventually, you're doing so
well that the company opens up

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.160
a data center.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.930
Next thing you know,
you're international

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.000
and spending billions
of dollars opening data

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.380
centers all around the world.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:00.990
Sounds like a lot
of work, right?

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.200
Here's the good news.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.230
You don't have to
do any of that.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.330
Luckily, some companies
have spent years

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.490
building some of the world's
most advanced networks

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:09.630
of data centers.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.490
Cloud computing is
when these companies

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.360
allow developers to store data
and run code on their computing

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.830
infrastructure.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.780
Google is one of
these companies.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.790
Google has data centers
all around the world

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.340
that have allowed it to
scale to billions of users.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.860
These data centers house
the computing infrastructure

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.990
that powers the Google products
you may be familiar with,

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.920
such as Search, Gmail,
and Google Photos.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.840
Google Cloud gives
you the access

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.700
to the same technology
and infrastructure

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.170
that powers Google.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.050
This means that Google Cloud
has over 100 products and tools

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.350
in categories like compute,
storage, and machine learning

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.710
and artificial intelligence that
seamlessly integrate together

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.510
all a click away.

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.370
One category is compute.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.310
These products help you
deploy code to the cloud.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.120
For example, we may use a
compute product like App Engine

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.650
to deploy a social
networking site.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.200
Another category is
storage and databases.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.550
These products help us store
data, retrieve, and query data

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.210
in the cloud.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.020
For example, we may want to
store data for our dog walking

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.300
app, the dogs'
names and locations,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.800
in a Cloud Firestore database.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.490
We also have machine learning
and artificial intelligence.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.950
These products allow us
to leverage Google's AI

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.860
technologies for our
own applications.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.600
We can build and
deploy our machine

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.290
learning models to AI Platform
or take advantage of one

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.840
of the easy-to-use
pre-trained machine learning

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.700
APIs like Vision or
Natural Language.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.790
So what is Google Cloud?

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.640
In a sentence, Google Cloud
lets developers build and host

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.270
applications and websites,
store data, and analyze data,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.970
all on Google's scalable
and reliable computing

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:46.540
infrastructure.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.870
Now, let's see what
some student developers

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.700
have done with Google Cloud.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.610
A team of student developers
called Guardian worked together

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:58.110
to help Alzheimer's patients
with a GPS-enabled smartwatch.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.230
The Guardian team leveraged a
lot of the Firebase platform

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.550
for their application.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.920
Firebase is Google Cloud's
comprehensive mobile

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.720
development platform.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.400
The team used Firebase
authentication

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.700
to easily verify App users,
Firebase Cloud Messaging

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.400
to send push notifications
to different platforms,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.670
and Cloud Functions for
Firebase to execute back

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.380
end code without having
to manage a server.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.440
They also used Cloud Firestore,
a Google Cloud database,

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.480
to store and sync user data.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.250
A team of student
developers created

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.950
a service called
Nagish to help people

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:31.710
who are deaf or hard of
hearing talk on the phone.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.690
They leveraged Google
Cloud's Text-to-Speech

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.210
and Speech-to-Text APIs
to convert back and forth

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.830
between voice and text.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.340
And that's it.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.630
Let's review.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.500
We started by talking
about what Google Cloud is,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.590
a platform that lets developers
build and host applications

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.530
and websites, store
data, and analyze data,

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.990
all on Google's scalable
and reliable computing

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.640
infrastructure.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:56.190
Then we discussed some of
the incredible projects

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:59.220
that student developers were
able to build with these tools

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.100
when working to learn skills
for their future careers.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.720
My name is Arman Hezarkhani.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.510
And I can't wait to see what
you build with Google Cloud.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

